    "text": "{\"anomaly_detected\": true, \"confidence\": 0.6, \"anomalies\": [{\"category\": \"polarity\", \"components\": [\"D1\", \"R6\", \"R4\"], \"pins\": [\"D1.1\", \"D1.2\", \"R6.2\", \"R4.1\"], \"nets\": [\"GND\", \"TACH\"], \"observed\": \"D1 (5V1 zener, D_Zener_Small) has its cathode (pin K, pin 1) on GND and its anode (pin A, pin 2) on TACH. R6 (10K) pulls TACH up to VCC. The clamp therefore sits in forward conduction whenever TACH is high, holding the node near 0.7 V rather than clamping an over-voltage excursion at 5.1 V.\", \"expected\": \"A zener used to clamp a 5 V tach input should have its cathode on the signal net (TACH) and its anode on GND, so it is reverse-biased and only conducts above the Zener voltage. With the orientation reversed, the pull-up R6 cannot raise TACH above about 0.7 V, so the tach input is stuck low and the fan speed signal is lost.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"D1\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pinfunction \\\"K\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"D1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"R6\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"5\\\") (name \\\"GND\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\")\"}], \"impact\": \"The tach signal at the MCU input (PB4 on U2/U3) never rises to a valid logic high. Tach or RPM feedback is lost, and firmware that depends on the tach pulses will misbehave.\", \"confidence\": 0.55}], \"summary\": \"D1 is wired with its cathode on GND and its anode on the TACH node, which is the reverse of a clamp that protects the tach input. Combined with the 10K pull-up R6, this holds TACH near a forward-biased diode drop, so the tach feedback path is broken. The specification does not describe this circuit, so the finding rests on standard practice and the netlist alone.\"}",
